  "sources": [
    "style-research ch.1 §1 Trap (mainstream 2010s–2020s) — the half-time skeleton crunk is the ancestor of",
    "style-research ch.1 §7 Memphis / Phonk — the neighbouring 130–150 double-count grammar",
    "style-research ch.3 Lex Luger — the 2000s Atlanta lineage's brass-and-snare-roll vocabulary",
    "style-research ch.3 Mike WiLL Made-It (flagship) — fat clap stacks on 3, restrained hats, negative space",
    "style-research ch.3 Zaytoven (flagship) — the Atlanta organ-and-keys lane crunk shares a city with",
    "style-research ch.2 §1 Trap melodies — minor/Phrygian split and static-i harmony",
    "AllMusic style entry — Crunk (Dirty South): chant hooks, minimal synth-brass riffs, 808-led minimalism",
    "Wikipedia — Crunk: 2000s Atlanta/Memphis club rap, ~70–80 BPM felt, call-and-response chant form"
  ],
  "notes": "Trap with the detail taken back out. The kick is heavy and deliberate rather than syncopated — 1 and 3 on the quarters, two or three hits a bar and no more — under a huge layered clap on the half-time 3. The hats are plain 8ths that stop and start rather than a 16th carpet, and rolls are rare enough to be an event. The 808 is the loudest and least melodic in the dataset: long, static, barely ever sliding. Harmony is a one- or two-chord minor vamp of bare triads, and the melody is a short synth-brass or organ stab riff sitting in the middle of the keyboard. All of that emptiness is deliberate — the record is built around a chant, and the chant needs the room. Confidence is medium because the compendium covers crunk only through its descendant (ch.1 §1) and its neighbour (ch.1 §7); the tempo band, the chant-space density and the flat triad harmony are extrapolated from those plus the public genre references cited above.",
